New Achievement Unlocked: Wife's Moose Obtained!

My beautiful and outrageously talented wife got her once-in-a-lifetime bull moose! She more often than not unsurprisingly impresses me and makes me proud to be her husband, and she once again did so that day as well. It was 09 October 2026.

(I would like to note something here: I am far more of a fisherman than a hunter. My wife is a fisherman as well, but she's by far more a hunter.)

It was a chilly Friday morning and rather than going up the mountains on the horses we'd decided to just drive out on the trail a bit in our trucks to a couple of spots where bull moose had been spotted numerous times so far. The MIL (mother in law) had snapped photos of a couple of bulls hanging out around a spot just four days prior and my wife picked that spot for our little outpost for the morning. She (my wife) was the only one with a moose tag, we (Mr BW, myself, and FIL (father in law)) were just spotters at this point.

The sun was still coming up and out of the corner of my eye I noticed what I thought was a heifer (as in beef cow, there's a lot of free range cattle here in Wyoming) moving across a grass spot in the willows when I said to myself, "Man, it's a bit early for a heifer to be on the move..Oh!" And told my wife I spotted something. Before I could get my binos up to my face she'd already done so, confirmed it was a bull moose 🫎 , exclaimed He's the one!, and was silently out of the truck with her rifle and stalking towards a firing position. 

It took her a few moments to scoot around and try to line up a shot she was happy with but couldn't find one using her bipod. Despite not being able to bench up for the good shot that she wanted, and having noticed the bull was now broadside to her and only (approximately) 120 yards away, she stood and took the shot from a free standing position (the most difficult and least stable firing position) and about 7:15 AM she pulled the trigger on the first of two shots. 

All said and done he was an 8x8 bull moose with a paddle (antler) width of 38.5 inches, and was at least 5 years old (Wyoming has some of his teeth and will notify us once they're processed). And a lot of meat. 

After we had all taken some momento photos we started quartering up the carcass, sent the old men (it's fine, they'll never read this😂) to camp for a couple of horses to haul the meat to the trucks, and the whole ordeal was done at 11:45 AM (save processing the meat which we'll be doing on and off over the next week or two). 

Of course, as we tend to do, FIL cooked a moose tenderloin on the grill that evening, along with the heart. (I'm not a big fan of eating organs, but a moose heart is quite delicious once you get over the leanness and texture, 5/7) 

And in case anyone is wondering, moose is in fact a quite healthy meat and is absolutely delicious. We harvested all four quarters, the back straps, tenderloins, as much of the rib and neck meat as possible, and his heart. It's a lot of meat, more than a single horse can carry in a trip, and we'll be processing all of that meat ourselves once back home. It's a family event for us here, and we'll hopefully have to do it a few times before the year ends.

I'll post pictures below of him after the kill and the spot he was harvested from, once I edit them for OpSec purposes. I couldn't be more proud of her, despite never doubting her. It's a truly awesome event to behold. 

=> /files/mindyfathermoose.png	My wife, her father, and her bull moose 09Oct26

Not whole lot to write about otherwise. Hunting camp and this demented sleeping schedule, plus barely being home long enough to do anything but preparing to return to camp .. yeah. Working on an alternative world for the Savage Worlds setting Deadlands based on The Hunt: Showdown, which is a recent development. And there's not much else. 

Still editing this gemlog on Android (using Markor) inside a local capsule (accessed via CX File Explorer) and live tested on a Gemeaux server which I'll sync to the VPS via CX File Explorer once I'm back in civilization with some amount of connectivity. I have zero connectivity at camp (which I prefer to be honest; touching grass should only count if you have no bars!).

Deus vult.

